hen there's character comedy which  simply comes from characters reacting  the way that character would react and  so in this movie I feel like the  character comedy is really good that's  the stuff that felt the most natural and  that made me laugh the hardest all the  stuff with Paul Rudd's crew it's like  those guys are kind of kooky characters  and because of that because the movie  establishes there were these kind of  quirky guys just their worldview is  funny and the way they behave is funny  um and that stuff really works but there  are times just kind of at the end of  scenes it feels like they'll tack on a  joke or like a funny quip for hell rug  to say and those were really hitting  this for me like none of them were bad  but a lot of them just didn't hit and  kind of felt a little awkward so that's  just kind of obscene where things stores  start to come apart a little bit  sometimes the humor and the heist in the  superhero the action are all working  together and other times they're sort of  coming apart or one is being favored  more than the other in a way that  doesn't quite work I know I'm gonna take  flack for this but that's the one place  where I really miss Edgar Wright on this  he's credited with the script and stuff  like that him and Joe Cornish I don't  know how far this draft is from theirs  but uh that's where I really miss Edgar  Wright is I feel like had Edgar Wright  been able to direct this movie he's a  master at spinning several genre plates  at once in keeping them all going it's  like if you watch hot fuzz hot fuzz has  elements of action comedy horror various  things and that movie those all fused  watching this I felt sort of the weight  of his absence every time the genres  would come apart basically Peyton Reed  definitely brings some shops to the  table there is some good time in here  there are some really creative visual  sequences especially what Michael Pena  is like telling Paul Rudd the intimate  details of like what he 